# Progressive loading (Suspense-style)

Show layout immediately. Load sections independently. Use skeletons until each resolves.

## Server `load` — do not block on independent fetches

- **Await only critical data** that must be in initial HTML (job list/count, detail body — SEO)
- **Return unresolved promises** for non-critical data (`getFilterOptions`, `listSavedSearches`, category slug map)
- **Never** `await Promise.all([listJobs(), getFilterOptions(), ...])` — slowest query gates the entire page

```ts
// ❌ BAD — everything waits for the slowest query
const [result, options, saved] = await Promise.all([
  listJobs(filters),
  getFilterOptions(),
  listSavedSearches(id)
]);

// ✅ GOOD — only block on jobs; stream the rest
const result = await listJobs(filters);
return {
  ...result,
  options: getFilterOptions(),
  savedSearches: listSavedSearches(id)
};
```

`Promise.all` inside a single logical unit (e.g. `findMany` + `count` in `listJobs`) is fine.

## UI — boundaries, skeletons, navigating

- Wrap each independently-loading section in `<svelte:boundary pending={skeleton}>`
- Use shadcn `Skeleton` shaped like real content — not spinners or plain "Loading…"
- On filter/pagination navigation, show skeletons when `$app/state` `navigating` is set (instant feedback on click)
- `{#await promise}` inside boundaries for explicit loading/error branches

```svelte
{#snippet filterSkeleton()}
  <FilterPanelSkeleton />
{/snippet}

<svelte:boundary pending={filterSkeleton}>
  {#await data.options}
    <FilterPanelSkeleton />
  {:then options}
    <FilterPanel {options} />
  {/await}
</svelte:boundary>
```

## Split components

- Extract `JobList`, `FilterPanel`, `*Skeleton` child components — one boundary per section
- No client `fetch` / `/api` for page data that `load` can stream
- Fire-and-forget side effects (`logSearch`) — never `await` in `load`
